...by which I mean "I wrote another book", proving to myself that the [[first time|Finishing a book]] wasn't a fluke.

I finished this one on 2026-10-03, or a little under two weeks ago -- fresh enough that I haven't started editing yet (I'll probably give myself 1-2 more weekends of "vacation" before breaking out the red pen). They say [[second novels are harder|https://jerichowriters.com/second-novel-syndrome-the-disease-the-symptoms-the-cure/]] but for me this one came easier:

[img[$:/images/i-did-it-again-1.png]]

And yes I know they typically mean second //published// novel, but I'd argue that aspiring authors are no less susceptible to the symptoms. I also had to deal with the fun of writing a story set in the present day (vs. the sci-fi setting of my first book), which required a lot more research than I bargained for. Still, you can see that progress remained steady throughout the 495 days and 62 commits that it took to write the ~105000 words.

While my feelings of accomplishment aren't as strong this time around, #2 will forever remain special to me because of how its journey was bookended: I both started and ended its writing on international flights (to and from Taipei, respectively). This by itself isn't so unusual -- I also started #1 on a flight to Venice, and write longhand on pretty much every plane trip I take -- but then I should clarify that the "to Taipei" trip happened in 2018. At the time I was trying to get back into writing and managed about 11000 words before fizzling out, at least until I picked up the threads of #1 a few months later. So when I made plans to return to Taipei this year (for the first time in 8 years) I knew the stars could align if I kept a strong writing pace.

There's a lot I'm glossing over, but I'll save those details for future posts. For sure there's one about the magic of liminal spaces, i.e. why I write so much on trips. If I ever do end up publishing this book I'd also love to talk about the character that saved its plot and rekindled the narrative fire.

For now, though, rest! Then editing. Then, yes, maybe a serious attempt at publishing; 100k is a lot more sensible than 200k for a traditional debut. We'll see how that goes.